 17592186045417] [17592186511369 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511369 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511369 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "74caf0bf-cacd-5f28-aa72-d801b776ea5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511428 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511428 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511428 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511428 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511428 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cf8bd0d4-e2df-5b96-ba24-b2e56674f0f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511435 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511435 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511435 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511435 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511435 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dff10b37-1425-5f08-9969-ac5f0106b371" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511451 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511451 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511451 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511451 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511451 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511451 #edb/ref 1089 #edb/ref 17592186511453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511451 #edb/ref 1090 #uuid "a0690a2f-978b-5af9-8216-9a182879d18d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511453 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186511590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511453 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186511626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511453 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186511627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511453 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186511629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511453 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186511659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511453 #edb/ref 1085 "A campsite opens on the $1$st of May $(t=0)$ and closes after $8$ months. The number of customers in the campsite after $t$ months is modeled by a differentiable function $q$ for $0\\le t\\le 8.$ Some values for $q(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ (months) | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $q(t)$ (customers) | $0$ | $59$ | $120$ | $92$ | $108$ | $85$ | $47$ | $18$ | $0$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511453 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "1f7f0079-ead3-5b48-9ed7-038c428c6b95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511453 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186511590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511500 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511500 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166362" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511500 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511500 #edb/ref 1071 "From the given data, the best forward difference approximation for the rate at which the number of customers was changing on June $1$st is{{field-1}}\ncustomers per month." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511500 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "61246f8d-17fc-5cf4-84dc-d3e84d220ded" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511500 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that June $1$st corresponds to $t=1.$\n\nWe use the forward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t+h)-q(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible. In this case, we choose $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{q(1+1)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{120-59}{1} \\\\  & =61.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using a forward difference approximation, the rate of change in the number of customers on June $1$st is approximately $61$ customers per month." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511505 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511505 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511505 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511505 #edb/ref 1071 "Use your answer to part 3 to approximate the average number of customers at the campsite during the first $4$ months.\n\nThe average number of customers at the campsite over the first four months was\n{{field-1}}\n." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511505 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b1c7ad5-e07c-5b25-9e08-a2e2aa0ac237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511505 #edb/ref 1074 "The average value of a continuous function $q(t)$ on a closed interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\n{q}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ≈348.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{q}_{\\text{ave}}≈\\frac{348}{4-0}=87.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511513 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511513 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511513 #edb/ref 1071 "From the given data, the best backward difference approximation for the rate at which the number of customers was changing on June $1$st is{{field-1}}\ncustomers per month." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a6777e5-075d-540c-87f4-265fdb022242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511513 #edb/ref 1074 "Again, we use the fact that June $1$st corresponds to $t=1.$\n\nWe use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t)-q(t-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible. In this case, we choose $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{q(1)-q(1-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(1)-q(0)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{59-0}{1} \\\\  & =59.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using a backward difference approximation, the rate of change in the number of customers on June $1$st is approximately $59$ customers per month." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511535 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511535 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511535 #edb/ref 1071 "For $0\\le t\\le 8,$ the equation ${q}^{′}(t)=0$ has at least{{field-1}}\nsolution(s)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9de46d9e-0321-5243-8082-f7e3f4b58ad3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511535 #edb/ref 1074 "The solutions to ${q}^{′}(t)=0$ correspond to the stationary points of the graph of $q(t).$\n\nSketching a possible graph of $q(t),$ we get the following:\n\n![](images/2f/2fd9e08520f4a671a255f360e583140175650de856a12233962c99a064c4c582.png)\n\nFrom our sketch, we notice three stationary points located near $t=2,$ $t=3,$ and $t=4.$\n\nTherefore, the equation ${q}^{′}(t)=0$ has at least three solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166364" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511543 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511543 #edb/ref 1071 "Using a trapezoidal sum with two equal subintervals, ${\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈${{field-1}}\n." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae9e49ed-eef5-5e75-8ccc-b6af327fb845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511543 #edb/ref 1074 "The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈\\frac{1}{2}[q(a)+2(q({t}_{1})+q({t}_{2})+…+q({t}_{n-1}))+q(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n* The number of trapezoids is $n=2.$\n\n* The corresponding interval is $[0,4],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=4.$\n\n* The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{4-0}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈\\frac{1}{2}[q(0)+2q(2)+q(4)]\\cdot 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[0+2(120)+108]\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =240+108 \\\\  & =348.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511590 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511590 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "14dc92fe-b926-56a3-9447-0731e156f799" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511590 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511609 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "367ea342-8ab9-51eb-810a-0e21d53f53dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511609 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511626 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511626 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65e9a74f-d4af-5e85-9717-677667dca31c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511626 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511627 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511627 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6719078a-ae01-5b15-a69d-2c93b4d15fad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511627 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511629 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511629 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "766b9e6a-e65b-53da-b6e9-5f712795eacf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511629 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c355678b-004a-527a-95e3-0498f895348c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511535 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
